// CHAPTER 2: Careers and Career Choices (Term 1)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Careers and Career Choices\n\nGrade 10 is the ideal time to begin thinking seriously about your future career. The subjects you chose for the FET phase (Grades 10-12) already point toward certain career paths. This chapter introduces career decision-making, career fields, and the importance of knowing your own interests and abilities.\n\n### Career Decision-Making\n\nCareer decision-making is the process of choosing a career that aligns with your interests, abilities, values, and personality. It is not a single decision but an ongoing process.\n\n**Steps in career decision-making:**\n1. **Self-assessment:** Understand your interests, strengths, values, and personality type\n2. **Career exploration:** Research different careers, job requirements, and opportunities\n3. **Narrowing your options:** Match your self-assessment results with suitable careers\n4. **Planning and preparation:** Choose the right subjects, get relevant experience, and plan your post-school education\n5. **Taking action:** Apply for programmes, bursaries, or jobs\n6. **Evaluating and adjusting:** Your career path may change — be flexible and open to new opportunities'),
  t(2, '### Career Fields and Clusters\n\nCareers can be grouped into broad fields or clusters. Understanding these helps you explore options you may not have considered:\n\n| Career field | Examples of careers | Related school subjects |\n|-------------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n| **Science and Technology** | Engineer, doctor, IT specialist, pharmacist | Maths, Physical Science, Life Sciences, IT |\n| **Business and Commerce** | Accountant, marketing manager, entrepreneur, banker | Accounting, Business Studies, Economics, Maths |\n| **Arts and Culture** | Graphic designer, journalist, musician, filmmaker | Visual Arts, Dramatic Arts, English, Music |\n| **Education and Training** | Teacher, lecturer, educational psychologist | Any subject — passion for teaching is key |\n| **Health and Social Services** | Nurse, social worker, psychologist, paramedic | Life Sciences, Maths, Physical Science |\n| **Agriculture and Environment** | Farmer, environmental scientist, conservation officer | Agricultural Sciences, Geography, Life Sciences |\n| **Manufacturing and Engineering** | Mechanical engineer, welder, electrician, artisan | Maths, Physical Science, Technical subjects |\n| **Services** | Chef, tourism manager, hairdresser, pilot | Tourism, Hospitality, Consumer Studies |'),
  q(3, 'Thandi loves Biology and wants to help people. Which career field would BEST suit her interests?',
    ['Health and Social Services', 'Business and Commerce', 'Manufacturing and Engineering', 'Arts and Culture'], 0,
    'Health and Social Services includes careers like nursing, social work, and psychology that combine a passion for biology/life sciences with a desire to help people.'),
  t(4, '### Identifying Your Own Interests, Abilities, and Strengths\n\nBefore choosing a career, you need to understand yourself:\n\n**Interests:** What do you enjoy doing? What subjects excite you? What topics do you read about or watch videos about in your free time?\n\n**Abilities:** What are you naturally good at? This includes academic skills, practical skills (building, fixing, creating), and interpersonal skills (leading, communicating, mediating).\n\n**Values:** What matters most to you? Examples: helping others, earning money, being creative, working outdoors, having job security, making a difference in your community.\n\n**Personality type:** Are you an introvert or extrovert? Do you prefer working alone or in teams? Are you detail-oriented or big-picture focused? Do you prefer structure or flexibility?\n\n**Self-assessment tools:**\n- Interest inventories and personality questionnaires (available at school career guidance offices)\n- The Khetha Career Guidance Booklet (free from the Department of Higher Education and Training)\n- Online tools at www.careerhelp.org.za (National Career Advice Portal)\n- Career expos and job shadowing opportunities'),
  t(5, '### Own Subjects and Career Links\n\nThe subjects you are taking in Grade 10 directly influence your post-school options. Here are some key links:\n\n**Mathematics (not Maths Literacy)** is required for:\n- Engineering, medicine, actuarial science, architecture, computer science, chartered accounting\n\n**Physical Science** is required for:\n- Medicine, engineering, pharmacy, veterinary science, chemistry, physics\n\n**Accounting** is required for:\n- Chartered accounting, financial management, auditing, tax consulting\n\n**Life Sciences** is useful for:\n- Nursing, physiotherapy, dentistry, environmental science, agriculture\n\n**Important notes:**\n- Maths Literacy limits your options — it is not accepted for most university degrees\n- Some careers require specific subject combinations (e.g., medicine requires Maths AND Physical Science AND Life Sciences)\n- Check university entrance requirements NOW so you can ensure you meet them\n- If you are unsure, speak to your school\'s career guidance teacher or contact the DHET helpline: 0800 872 222'),
  fb(6, 'The free career guidance resource from the Department of Higher Education and Training is called the ___ Booklet. The National Career Advice Portal website is www.___.',
    ['Khetha', 'careerhelp.org.za'],
    'The Khetha Career Guidance Booklet is provided free by the DHET to help learners with career planning. The National Career Advice Portal at www.careerhelp.org.za provides career information and guidance.'),
  t(7, '### Developing a Career Portfolio\n\nA career portfolio is a collection of documents and information that tracks your career exploration journey:\n\n**What to include:**\n- Results of self-assessment activities (interest inventories, personality tests)\n- Your current school report and subject choices\n- Information about careers you are interested in\n- Pamphlets or notes from career expos or open days\n- A list of your skills, achievements, and extramural activities\n- Your personal goals — short-term (this year) and long-term (5-10 years)\n- Contact information for career guidance resources\n\n**Why it matters:**\n- Organises your thinking about your future\n- Helps you track how your interests and goals evolve over Grades 10-12\n- Provides material for future CV writing and bursary applications\n- Shows universities and employers that you are proactive and organised\n\nStart your career portfolio NOW. Even a simple folder with printed or handwritten notes is a good beginning.'),
  q(8, 'Why is it important to check university entrance requirements while still in Grade 10?',
    ['So you can ensure your current subject choices meet the requirements for your desired career', 'University requirements never change, so it does not matter when you check', 'Grade 10 marks determine university admission', 'Universities do not have entrance requirements'], 0,
    'University entrance requirements specify which subjects (and sometimes minimum marks) are needed for admission to specific programmes. Checking in Grade 10 gives you time to change subjects if necessary.'),
  t(9, '### Steps in Choosing a Career Field\n\nChoosing a career field is a structured process:\n\n**Step 1: Know yourself**\n- Complete self-assessment activities\n- Identify your interests, abilities, values, and personality\n\n**Step 2: Explore options**\n- Research careers within fields that match your profile\n- Attend career expos, open days, and information sessions\n- Talk to people working in careers you are interested in\n- Use the Khetha Booklet and online resources\n\n**Step 3: Narrow your choices**\n- Compare your profile with career requirements\n- Consider practical factors: duration of study, cost, job availability, location\n- Discuss with parents, teachers, and career counsellors\n\n**Step 4: Plan your path**\n- Ensure your subject choices support your career goals\n- Research post-school institutions (universities, UoTs, TVET colleges)\n- Look into bursaries and financial aid options (NSFAS, private bursaries)\n\n**Step 5: Stay flexible**\n- Your interests may change as you grow — that is normal\n- Some people change careers several times in their lifetime\n- Focus on developing transferable skills that work across multiple careers'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following is NOT a step in the career decision-making process?',
    ['Choosing the career that pays the most without considering your interests', 'Completing a self-assessment of your interests and abilities', 'Researching careers that match your profile', 'Discussing options with parents and career counsellors'], 0,
    'Career decision-making should consider interests, abilities, values, and personality alongside practical factors like salary. Choosing a career based solely on income without considering personal fit often leads to dissatisfaction.'),
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// CHAPTER 3: Democracy and Human Rights (Term 1)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Democracy and Human Rights\n\nSouth Africa is a constitutional democracy — a system of government in which the Constitution is the supreme law and the rights of all citizens are protected. Understanding your rights and responsibilities is essential for active, responsible citizenship.\n\n### The South African Constitution and the Bill of Rights\n\nThe Constitution of the Republic of South Africa (1996) is the highest law of the land. Any law or action that contradicts the Constitution is invalid.\n\n**Key features of the Constitution:**\n- Establishes a democratic government based on the rule of law\n- Contains the Bill of Rights (Chapter 2), which protects fundamental human rights\n- Creates three branches of government: Legislature (makes laws), Executive (implements laws), Judiciary (interprets laws)\n- Guarantees an independent judiciary and free media\n- Applies to all people in South Africa — citizens and non-citizens alike'),
  t(2, '### The Bill of Rights — Key Rights\n\nThe Bill of Rights (Chapter 2, Sections 7-39) lists the fundamental rights of all people in South Africa:\n\n| Section | Right | What it means |\n|---------|-------|---------------|\n| **Section 9** | Equality | Everyone is equal before the law; no unfair discrimination |\n| **Section 10** | Human dignity | Everyone has inherent dignity and the right to have it respected |\n| **Section 11** | Life | Everyone has the right to life |\n| **Section 12** | Freedom and security of the person | No torture, no violence, bodily integrity |\n| **Section 14** | Privacy | Right to privacy of person, home, property, communications |\n| **Section 15** | Freedom of religion, belief and opinion | Everyone can choose their own religion and beliefs |\n| **Section 16** | Freedom of expression | Freedom of speech, press, and artistic creativity — but NOT hate speech |\n| **Section 24** | Environment | Right to an environment not harmful to health or well-being |\n| **Section 28** | Children | Right to basic nutrition, shelter, healthcare, social services, education, protection from abuse |\n| **Section 29** | Education | Everyone has the right to basic education |'),
  q(3, 'Which of the following is NOT protected by the right to freedom of expression (Section 16)?',
    ['Hate speech that incites harm against a group based on race or religion', 'Expressing your political opinion peacefully', 'Publishing a newspaper article criticising the government', 'Creating artistic works that challenge social norms'], 0,
    'Section 16 protects freedom of expression but explicitly excludes propaganda for war, incitement of imminent violence, and advocacy of hatred based on race, ethnicity, gender, or religion that constitutes incitement to cause harm.'),
  t(4, '### Discrimination and Violations of Human Rights\n\nDiscrimination occurs when a person is treated unfairly because of a characteristic such as race, gender, disability, religion, or sexual orientation.\n\n**Forms of discrimination in South Africa:**\n\n| Form | Description | Example |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| **Racism** | Discrimination based on race or skin colour | A shop owner following Black customers because they "look suspicious" |\n| **Sexism** | Discrimination based on sex or gender | Paying women less than men for the same job |\n| **Xenophobia** | Hatred or fear of foreign nationals | Attacks on foreign-owned shops in townships |\n| **Homophobia** | Discrimination against LGBTQI+ people | Bullying a learner because of their sexual orientation |\n| **Ableism** | Discrimination against people with disabilities | A school refusing to provide wheelchair access |\n| **Religious intolerance** | Discrimination based on religion | Mocking a learner\'s cultural or religious practices |\n\n**The impact of discrimination:**\n- Emotional harm: anxiety, depression, low self-esteem, trauma\n- Physical harm: hate crimes, gender-based violence, xenophobic attacks\n- Social harm: exclusion, isolation, division in communities\n- Economic harm: unequal access to jobs, education, and resources'),
  fb(5, 'Hatred or fear of foreign nationals is called ___. The chapter of the Constitution that contains the Bill of Rights is Chapter ___.',
    ['xenophobia', '2'],
    'Xenophobia is prejudice against people from other countries. The Bill of Rights is found in Chapter 2 of the South African Constitution.'),
  t(6, '### Human Rights Violations — Historical and Contemporary\n\n**Apartheid (1948-1994):** The most systematic violation of human rights in South African history:\n- Racial classification and forced separation of races\n- Pass laws restricted movement of Black South Africans\n- Forced removals from areas designated as "White" (e.g., District Six, Sophiatown)\n- Inferior "Bantu Education" designed to limit Black learners\n- Detention without trial, torture, and killings of activists\n- The Sharpeville Massacre (1960) and Soweto Uprising (1976) are key events\n\n**Contemporary human rights violations in South Africa:**\n- Gender-based violence (GBV): SA has one of the highest rates in the world\n- Xenophobic violence against foreign nationals\n- Corruption that denies citizens access to services\n- Human trafficking\n- Child abuse and neglect\n- Police brutality (e.g., Marikana, 2012)\n- Homophobic hate crimes, including "corrective rape"\n\n**Organisations that protect human rights in SA:**\n- South African Human Rights Commission (SAHRC)\n- Commission for Gender Equality (CGE)\n- Public Protector\n- Constitutional Court'),
  q(7, 'The Soweto Uprising of 16 June 1976 was a protest by learners against:',
    ['The imposition of Afrikaans as a medium of instruction in Black schools', 'The abolition of school sport', 'High school fees in Soweto', 'The closure of universities'], 0,
    'On 16 June 1976, thousands of learners in Soweto marched against the apartheid government\'s decree that Afrikaans be used as a medium of instruction in Black schools. The police opened fire, and the event became a turning point in the struggle against apartheid. Youth Day (16 June) commemorates this event.'),
  t(8, '### Contemporary Events Showcasing Human Rights\n\nSouth Africa\'s commitment to human rights is demonstrated through various commemorative days and campaigns:\n\n| Event/Day | Date | Significance |\n|-----------|------|-------------|\n| **Human Rights Day** | 21 March | Commemorates the Sharpeville Massacre (1960); celebrates the Bill of Rights |\n| **Freedom Day** | 27 April | Celebrates the first democratic elections (1994) |\n| **Youth Day** | 16 June | Commemorates the Soweto Uprising (1976) |\n| **Women\'s Day** | 9 August | Honours the 1956 Women\'s March to the Union Buildings against pass laws |\n| **16 Days of Activism** | 25 Nov – 10 Dec | Campaign against gender-based violence |\n| **Heritage Day** | 24 September | Celebrates South Africa\'s cultural diversity |\n\n**South African campaigns addressing discrimination:**\n- Desmond Tutu and the "Rainbow Nation" concept\n- Sophie de Bruin, Charlotte Maxeke, Helen Suzman, Albertina Sisulu — women who fought for rights\n- Brand Proudly SA — promoting national unity\n- Reconciliation Day (16 December) — healing divisions'),
  t(9, '### Responsible Citizenship\n\nHaving rights comes with responsibilities. A responsible citizen:\n\n**Knows their rights:** Understands what the Bill of Rights protects and how to claim those rights when violated.\n\n**Respects others\' rights:** Your rights end where another person\'s rights begin. Freedom of expression does not mean freedom to harm others.\n\n**Participates in democracy:**\n- Votes in elections (from age 18)\n- Participates in community meetings and public consultations\n- Joins organisations that promote human rights\n- Reports corruption and human rights violations\n\n**Challenges prejudice and discrimination:**\n- Speaks out against racism, sexism, xenophobia, and homophobia\n- Does not participate in discriminatory behaviour\n- Supports victims of discrimination\n\n**Takes action:**\n- One person CAN make a difference\n- Start in your school and community\n- Use social media responsibly to promote human rights\n- Contact the SAHRC if your rights are violated: 011 877 3600'),
  q(10, 'Sipho witnesses a group of learners bullying a foreign national learner at school. As a responsible citizen, what should he do?',
    ['Intervene safely or report it to a teacher, and support the learner being bullied', 'Ignore it because it is none of his business', 'Join in to fit in with the group', 'Record it on his phone and post it on social media'], 0,
    'Responsible citizenship means standing up against discrimination and protecting others\' rights. Reporting bullying to a teacher ensures the school can take appropriate action. Recording and posting the incident could further traumatise the victim and may violate their dignity.'),
  fb(11, 'The highest law of South Africa that protects all human rights is the ___. The South African Human Rights ___ is the body that investigates human rights complaints.',
    ['Constitution', 'Commission'],
    'The Constitution of the Republic of South Africa (1996) is the supreme law. The South African Human Rights Commission (SAHRC) is the constitutional body mandated to investigate human rights complaints.'),
];

