// CHAPTER 4: Social and Environmental Responsibility (Term 2)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Social and Environmental Responsibility\n\nAs a Grade 10 learner, you are becoming increasingly aware of social and environmental issues that affect your community, your country, and the world. This chapter explores contemporary social issues, the skills needed to participate in civic life, and environmental responsibility.\n\n### Contemporary Social Issues in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa faces a range of interconnected social challenges:\n\n**Poverty and inequality:**\n- SA is one of the most unequal countries in the world (high Gini coefficient)\n- Over 55% of South Africans live below the poverty line\n- The legacy of apartheid created structural inequality in access to land, education, healthcare, and economic opportunity\n- Poverty affects access to basic services: water, electricity, sanitation, and quality education\n\n**Unemployment:**\n- SA\'s unemployment rate is among the highest in the world (over 30%)\n- Youth unemployment is even higher (over 60% for those aged 15-24)\n- This contributes to crime, substance abuse, and hopelessness\n\n**Crime and violence:**\n- High rates of violent crime, including murder, robbery, and assault\n- Gender-based violence (GBV) is a national crisis\n- Gang violence affects many communities, particularly in the Western Cape'),
  t(2, '### More Social Issues Affecting Communities\n\n**Substance abuse:**\n- Alcohol abuse is a major contributor to crime, road accidents, and domestic violence\n- Drug abuse (tik, nyaope, dagga, mandrax) devastates families and communities\n- Substance abuse often begins in adolescence due to peer pressure, trauma, or lack of opportunity\n\n**Safety and security:**\n- Many communities lack adequate policing and street lighting\n- Schools face challenges with bullying, weapons, and substance abuse\n- Home security is a concern, particularly in informal settlements\n\n**Lack of basic services:**\n- Some communities still lack clean water, proper sanitation, and reliable electricity\n- Service delivery protests occur when communities feel their needs are being ignored\n- Health facilities in rural areas are often understaffed and under-resourced\n\n**Unequal access to resources:**\n- Quality education is unevenly distributed — well-resourced schools vs. under-resourced schools\n- Access to technology and the internet varies dramatically between urban and rural areas\n- Healthcare quality differs between public and private facilities\n\nThese issues are interconnected: poverty leads to poor education, which leads to unemployment, which leads to crime and substance abuse — creating a cycle that is difficult to break.'),
  q(3, 'Which of the following BEST explains why social issues like poverty, unemployment, and crime are described as "interconnected"?',
    ['Each issue causes or worsens the others, creating a cycle that is difficult to break', 'They are all caused by a single factor that is easy to fix', 'They affect different communities and have no relationship to each other', 'They only exist in South Africa and nowhere else in the world'], 0,
    'Social issues are interconnected because they form cycles: poverty limits access to education, poor education leads to unemployment, unemployment contributes to crime and substance abuse, which deepens poverty. Breaking these cycles requires addressing multiple issues simultaneously.'),
  t(4, '### Skills for Civic Participation\n\nCivic participation means being actively involved in your community and working to address social issues. Key skills include:\n\n**Social thinking skills:**\n- Analysing the root causes of social problems (not just symptoms)\n- Understanding different perspectives and viewpoints\n- Thinking critically about information in the media and social media\n\n**Constructive thinking skills:**\n- Finding practical solutions to problems\n- Planning and organising projects and campaigns\n- Evaluating the effectiveness of actions taken\n\n**Critical thinking skills:**\n- Questioning assumptions and stereotypes\n- Distinguishing between facts and opinions\n- Identifying bias in arguments and media\n\n**Problem-solving skills:**\n- Identifying the problem clearly\n- Generating possible solutions\n- Evaluating options and choosing the best course of action\n- Implementing the solution and reviewing results\n\nThese skills are not just academic — they are essential for participation in a democracy and for addressing the challenges facing South African communities.'),
  fb(5, 'Being actively involved in your community and working to address social issues is called ___ participation. South Africa\'s unemployment rate is over ___% overall.',
    ['civic', '30'],
    'Civic participation means engaging actively in the life of your community and country. South Africa has one of the highest unemployment rates in the world, consistently above 30%.'),
  t(6, '### Youth Participation in Community Development\n\nYoung people have a crucial role to play in community development:\n\n**How you can participate:**\n- **Youth organisations:** Join or start a youth club at school or in your community that addresses local issues\n- **Community service:** Volunteer at shelters, feeding schemes, or clean-up campaigns\n- **Community projects:** Identify a need in your area and organise a project to address it (e.g., a homework support programme, a food garden, or a recycling initiative)\n- **Civic structures:** Attend ward meetings, engage with your local councillor, or participate in school governing body elections\n\n**Examples of youth-driven change in SA:**\n- Equal Education campaigns for equal learning conditions in all schools\n- Section27 fights for the right to education and healthcare\n- Youth-led environmental clean-ups in coastal communities\n- Student-led campaigns against GBV on university campuses\n\n**Barriers to youth participation:**\n- Feeling that your voice does not matter\n- Lack of information about how to get involved\n- Safety concerns in some communities\n- Limited resources and support from adults\n\nRemember: the 1976 Soweto learners proved that young people can change history. Your generation has the same power.'),
  q(7, 'Which of the following is the BEST example of a youth-driven community development project?',
    ['Starting a homework support programme for younger learners in your community', 'Complaining about problems on social media without taking action', 'Waiting for the government to solve all community problems', 'Only focusing on your own schoolwork and ignoring community needs'], 0,
    'Starting a homework support programme directly addresses the educational challenges in a community. It is practical, uses the skills Grade 10 learners already have, and makes a real difference. Civic participation requires action, not just awareness.'),
  t(8, '### Environmental Responsibility\n\nSouth Africa\'s natural environment is under threat from human activity. Section 24 of the Bill of Rights gives everyone the right to an environment that is not harmful to their health or well-being.\n\n**Environmental challenges in South Africa:**\n\n| Challenge | Description | Impact |\n|-----------|-------------|--------|\n| **Water scarcity** | SA is a water-scarce country; droughts are increasing due to climate change | Affects agriculture, households, and industry |\n| **Pollution** | Air pollution from industry and vehicles; water pollution from mining and sewage | Respiratory diseases, unsafe drinking water |\n| **Deforestation** | Clearing of indigenous forests for farming, housing, and industry | Loss of biodiversity, soil erosion, climate change |\n| **Waste and littering** | Inadequate waste management; illegal dumping | Health hazards, pollution of rivers and oceans |\n| **Climate change** | Rising temperatures, extreme weather events, sea-level rise | Droughts, floods, crop failures, displacement |\n| **Poaching** | Illegal hunting of endangered species (rhinos, elephants, abalone) | Biodiversity loss, disruption of ecosystems |'),
  t(9, '### Taking Environmental Action\n\nEvery individual can contribute to protecting the environment:\n\n**At home:**\n- Save water: fix leaks, take shorter showers, collect rainwater\n- Save electricity: switch off lights, use energy-efficient bulbs, unplug devices\n- Reduce waste: avoid single-use plastics, recycle, compost food scraps\n- Plant indigenous trees and plants in your garden\n\n**At school:**\n- Start or join an eco-club\n- Organise recycling programmes and clean-up campaigns\n- Create awareness about environmental issues through assemblies and posters\n- Start a school vegetable garden\n\n**In your community:**\n- Participate in river, beach, or neighbourhood clean-ups\n- Report illegal dumping to your municipality\n- Support organisations like WESSA (Wildlife and Environment Society of SA)\n- Advocate for better waste management services\n\n**Government environmental programmes:**\n- Working for Water: removes invasive alien plants\n- Working on Fire: manages wildfire risk\n- SANParks: manages national parks and protected areas\n- DEA (Department of Environment, Forestry and Fisheries): enforces environmental legislation'),
  q(10, 'Section 24 of the South African Bill of Rights protects:',
    ['The right to an environment that is not harmful to health or well-being', 'The right to unlimited use of natural resources', 'The right to dump waste anywhere you choose', 'The right to own endangered animals as pets'], 0,
    'Section 24 guarantees the right to a clean and healthy environment and requires the state to take reasonable measures to prevent pollution, promote conservation, and ensure sustainable development for present and future generations.'),
];

