// CHAPTER 5: Study Skills and Study Methods (Term 2)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Study Skills and Study Methods\n\nGrade 10 marks the beginning of the FET phase, where the content becomes more demanding and the volume of work increases significantly. Developing effective study skills now will serve you throughout Grades 11 and 12 and beyond.\n\n### What Are Study Skills?\n\nStudy skills are the methods and strategies you use to learn, process, and remember information effectively. They are not talents you are born with — they are skills that can be learned and improved with practice.\n\n**Core study skills:**\n- **Listening:** Paying attention in class, taking notes, asking questions\n- **Reading:** Reading actively, highlighting key points, summarising\n- **Comprehension:** Understanding what you read, not just memorising words\n- **Concentration:** Focusing on your work without distractions\n- **Memory:** Using techniques to store and retrieve information\n- **Organisation:** Keeping notes, materials, and time well-organised\n- **Time management:** Planning when and how long to study each subject'),
  t(2, '### Study Methods\n\nDifferent study methods work for different people and different subjects. Here are the most effective, research-backed methods:\n\n**Note-taking:**\n- Write notes in your own words during class — do not just copy the textbook\n- Use the Cornell Method: divide your page into three sections (notes, cues, summary)\n- Review and add to your notes within 24 hours of the lesson\n\n**Mind-mapping:**\n- Start with the main topic in the centre\n- Branch out with subtopics, using colours and images\n- Great for subjects with many interconnected concepts (e.g., Life Sciences, History)\n\n**Summarising:**\n- Reduce large amounts of information to key points\n- Use bullet points, numbered lists, and tables\n- A good summary is short enough to review quickly before a test\n\n**Active recall:**\n- After studying a section, close your book and write down everything you remember\n- This is far more effective than re-reading notes\n- Use flashcards, practice questions, or teach someone else\n\n**Spaced repetition:**\n- Review material at increasing intervals: 1 day, 3 days, 7 days, 14 days\n- This moves information from short-term to long-term memory\n- Do NOT cram — it creates an illusion of learning that fades quickly'),
  q(3, 'Ayanda reads through her notes five times the night before a test. Why is this NOT an effective study strategy?',
    ['Re-reading creates a false sense of familiarity without testing actual understanding or recall', 'Five times is too many — once is enough', 'Reading is only effective for Maths', 'Night-time studying is always ineffective'], 0,
    'Re-reading creates the illusion of knowing the material because it feels familiar, but familiarity is not the same as understanding or being able to recall information in a test. Active recall (testing yourself) is far more effective.'),
  t(4, '### Time Management and Study Planning\n\nEffective study requires planning your time:\n\n**Creating a study timetable:**\n1. List all your subjects and upcoming assessments\n2. Identify which subjects need the most attention (usually your weakest subjects)\n3. Allocate specific time slots for each subject\n4. Study your most difficult subjects during your peak focus time (usually morning or early evening)\n5. Include breaks — study for 25-40 minutes, then take a 5-10 minute break\n6. Be realistic — do not plan 8 hours of studying if you know you will only manage 3\n\n**Tips for time management:**\n- Use a diary, planner, or phone calendar to track deadlines\n- Break large assignments into smaller tasks with mini-deadlines\n- Do not leave everything to the last minute — procrastination increases stress and reduces quality\n- Set specific goals for each study session ("Complete summary of Chapter 3" not just "Study Geography")\n- Reward yourself after completing study goals\n\n**The Pomodoro Technique:**\nStudy for 25 minutes, take a 5-minute break. After 4 cycles, take a 20-minute break. This technique prevents burnout and maintains focus.'),
  fb(5, 'The study method of testing yourself on material instead of re-reading is called ___. Reviewing material at increasing intervals to move it into long-term memory is called ___ repetition.',
    ['active recall', 'spaced'],
    'Active recall forces your brain to retrieve information, which strengthens memory. Spaced repetition distributes study sessions over time, which is proven to be far more effective than cramming.'),
  t(6, '### Concentration and Dealing with Distractions\n\nConcentration is the ability to focus your attention on a task. In the age of smartphones and social media, this is a critical skill:\n\n**Common distractions:**\n- Phone notifications (WhatsApp, Instagram, TikTok)\n- Noise from family members, neighbours, or television\n- Hunger, thirst, or tiredness\n- Worrying about other things (personal problems, social issues)\n- An uncomfortable or cluttered study space\n\n**Strategies to improve concentration:**\n- **Remove your phone** from your study area (or put it on airplane mode)\n- **Create a dedicated study space** that is quiet, well-lit, and tidy\n- **Eat and hydrate** before studying — a hungry brain cannot concentrate\n- **Get enough sleep** — teenagers need 8-10 hours per night for optimal brain function\n- **Exercise regularly** — physical activity improves focus and reduces stress\n- **Use background music wisely** — instrumental music may help some learners, but music with lyrics is distracting\n- **Practice mindfulness** — even 5 minutes of deep breathing before studying can improve focus\n\nIf you do not have a quiet study space at home, use the school library, a community library, or study with a friend at their home.'),
  q(7, 'Which of the following is the BEST study environment for effective concentration?',
    ['A quiet, well-lit space with phone on airplane mode and all materials ready', 'Studying in bed with the TV on for background noise', 'A noisy kitchen table with your phone next to you', 'A dark room late at night after insufficient sleep'], 0,
    'An optimal study environment is quiet, well-lit, free from distractions (especially phones), and prepared with all necessary materials. Physical comfort, adequate lighting, and minimal distractions are key to maintaining concentration.'),
  t(8, '### Assessment Preparation\n\nGrade 10 assessments include class tests, assignments, projects, practical tasks, and examinations. Effective preparation is essential:\n\n**Before the assessment:**\n- Start studying well in advance — not the night before\n- Understand the format: How many marks? How much time? What types of questions?\n- Practise past papers and mark schemes — this is one of the most effective preparation methods\n- Make a study checklist of all topics to cover and tick them off as you go\n\n**Understanding question types:**\n\n| Question type | What it requires |\n|--------------|------------------|\n| **Multiple choice** | Read ALL options before choosing; eliminate clearly wrong answers |\n| **True or false** | Look for absolute words ("always", "never") which are often false |\n| **Short answer** | Be concise and specific; use the mark allocation as a guide |\n| **Essay/Discuss** | Plan before writing; use paragraphs; include intro, body, conclusion |\n| **Case study** | Read the case carefully; refer back to it in your answers |\n\n**During the assessment:**\n- Read through the entire paper first\n- Start with questions you feel most confident about\n- Allocate time based on mark allocation (e.g., 60 marks in 60 minutes = 1 minute per mark)\n- Never leave a question blank — attempt every question'),
  t(9, '### Study Groups and Peer Learning\n\nStudying with others can be highly effective when done properly:\n\n**Benefits of study groups:**\n- Explaining concepts to others deepens your own understanding\n- You can learn from others\' strengths and perspectives\n- Study groups provide motivation and accountability\n- They break the isolation of studying alone\n\n**How to make study groups effective:**\n- Keep the group small (3-5 people)\n- Choose members who are committed and focused\n- Set a clear agenda for each session (specific topics or practice papers)\n- Take turns explaining concepts to each other\n- Do not let the session become a social gathering — stay on task\n- Set time limits for each topic\n\n**When study groups do NOT work:**\n- When members are unprepared and rely on others\n- When the group gets distracted by socialising\n- When one person dominates and others do not participate\n- When members are at very different levels of understanding\n\nRemember: study groups supplement individual study — they do not replace it. Do your own preparation before group sessions.'),
  q(10, 'What is the most effective way to prepare for a Grade 10 examination?',
    ['Start studying early, use active recall, practise past papers, and create a study timetable', 'Read through notes once the night before the exam', 'Only study the topics your friends say are important', 'Highlight every line in the textbook without summarising'], 0,
    'Effective exam preparation combines early planning (study timetable), evidence-based techniques (active recall), practice (past papers), and consistent effort over time. Last-minute cramming and passive re-reading are ineffective approaches.'),
];

