// CHAPTER 6: Adolescence and Development (Term 3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Adolescence and Development\n\nAdolescence is the transition from childhood to adulthood, typically between ages 10 and 19. As a Grade 10 learner, you are in the middle of this journey. Understanding the changes you are experiencing — physical, emotional, and social — helps you navigate this period with confidence and make responsible choices.\n\n### Life Roles\n\nEveryone plays multiple roles in life, and these roles change over time:\n\n| Role | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Student** | Learning, completing assignments, preparing for your future |\n| **Member of a family** | Son/daughter, sibling, grandchild — with responsibilities at home |\n| **Friend** | Supporting, listening, spending time with peers |\n| **Community member** | Contributing to your neighbourhood, school, and broader community |\n| **Citizen** | Exercising your rights and responsibilities in a democracy |\n| **Future employee/entrepreneur** | Preparing for your career through education and skill development |\n\n**Understanding your roles helps you:**\n- Manage competing demands (schoolwork vs. family vs. friends)\n- Set priorities and allocate your time wisely\n- Understand the responsibilities that come with each role\n- Prepare for new roles you will take on as you grow (partner, parent, employee, leader)'),
  t(2, '### Physical Changes During Adolescence\n\nAdolescence involves significant physical changes driven by hormones:\n\n**Primary changes in the body:**\n- Rapid physical growth (growth spurts) — bones grow longer, muscles develop\n- Development of secondary sexual characteristics\n- Hormonal changes that affect mood, energy, and body functioning\n- Maturation of reproductive organs\n\n**Changes in males:**\n- Voice deepens (breaking of the voice)\n- Growth of facial and body hair\n- Broadening of shoulders and increased muscle mass\n- Production of sperm begins (wet dreams are normal)\n- Skin changes (acne) due to increased oil production\n\n**Changes in females:**\n- Breast development\n- Widening of hips\n- Growth of body hair\n- Onset of menstruation (periods) — the menstrual cycle typically lasts 28 days\n- Skin changes (acne) due to hormonal shifts\n\n**Important to remember:**\n- Everyone develops at their own pace — there is no "right" timeline\n- These changes are completely normal and natural\n- If you have concerns about your development, speak to a trusted adult or healthcare professional'),
  q(3, 'Which of the following statements about adolescent development is TRUE?',
    ['Everyone develops at their own pace, and there is a wide range of normal', 'All teenagers go through puberty at exactly the same age', 'Physical changes during puberty are a sign that something is wrong', 'Only females experience emotional changes during adolescence'], 0,
    'Adolescent development varies widely — some teenagers start puberty earlier, others later. This variation is completely normal. Both males and females experience physical and emotional changes during this period.'),
  t(4, '### Emotional Changes During Adolescence\n\nAdolescence brings intense emotional experiences:\n\n**Common emotional changes:**\n- **Mood swings:** Feeling happy one moment and sad or irritable the next, often due to hormonal changes\n- **Heightened emotions:** Feelings of love, anger, excitement, and sadness may be more intense than before\n- **Self-consciousness:** Increased awareness of how others perceive you; worrying about fitting in\n- **Desire for independence:** Wanting to make your own decisions and establish your identity separate from parents\n- **Emotional sensitivity:** Being more easily hurt by criticism or rejection\n- **Romantic feelings:** Experiencing attraction and romantic interest\n\n**Coping with emotional changes:**\n- Recognise that mood swings are normal during adolescence\n- Talk to someone you trust about how you are feeling\n- Use healthy outlets: exercise, journaling, creative activities, music\n- Practise deep breathing or mindfulness when feeling overwhelmed\n- Avoid unhealthy coping mechanisms: substance abuse, aggression, isolation\n- If you feel persistently sad, anxious, or hopeless, seek help — SADAG: 0800 567 567'),
  fb(5, 'The transition from childhood to adulthood is called ___. The hormonal changes during this period often cause emotional ups and downs known as mood ___.',
    ['adolescence', 'swings'],
    'Adolescence is the developmental stage between childhood and adulthood. Mood swings — rapid changes in emotional state — are common during this period due to hormonal fluctuations.'),
  t(6, '### Social Changes and Peer Pressure\n\nAdolescence is a time when social relationships become increasingly important:\n\n**Key social changes:**\n- Friendships become deeper and more complex\n- Peer groups have a stronger influence on behaviour, dress, language, and values\n- Interest in romantic relationships develops\n- The desire to belong and be accepted intensifies\n- Conflict with parents may increase as you seek more independence\n\n**Peer pressure:**\nPeer pressure is the influence your age group has on your behaviour and decisions. It can be positive or negative:\n\n| Positive peer pressure | Negative peer pressure |\n|-----------------------|------------------------|\n| Encouraging you to study harder | Pressuring you to use drugs or alcohol |\n| Motivating you to participate in sport | Encouraging you to bunk school |\n| Supporting your goals and dreams | Mocking you for being responsible |\n| Setting a good example through their behaviour | Pressuring you into sexual activity |\n\n**How to resist negative peer pressure:**\n- Say "No" clearly and firmly\n- Walk away from situations that make you uncomfortable\n- Surround yourself with friends who share your values\n- Prepare responses in advance for common pressure situations\n- Remember: true friends will respect your decisions\n- It takes courage to stand alone — but it is a sign of maturity, not weakness'),
  q(7, 'Bongani\'s friends are pressuring him to try dagga at a party. They say, "Everyone does it — don\'t be a baby." Which strategy would be MOST effective for Bongani?',
    ['Saying "No" firmly, walking away, and spending time with friends who respect his choices', 'Trying it once to stop the teasing and then stopping', 'Pretending to be sick to avoid the situation without confronting it', 'Going along with it to maintain his friendships'], 0,
    'Assertively saying "No," removing himself from the situation, and choosing friends who respect his decisions are the most effective strategies. Trying it "just once" is how many addictions begin, and avoiding the situation without addressing it does not build the skills to handle future pressure.'),
  t(8, '### Sexuality and Responsible Choices\n\nSexuality is a normal part of human development. Making responsible choices about sexuality is one of the most important aspects of adolescence:\n\n**Understanding sexuality:**\n- Sexuality includes feelings of attraction, romantic interest, and sexual desire\n- Sexual orientation describes who you are attracted to:\n  - Heterosexual: attracted to the opposite sex\n  - Homosexual (gay/lesbian): attracted to the same sex\n  - Bisexual: attracted to both sexes\n  - All sexual orientations are protected by Section 9 of the Constitution\n\n**Responsible sexual choices:**\n- **Abstinence:** Choosing not to have sex. This is the only 100% effective method of preventing pregnancy and STIs. There is no shame in choosing abstinence.\n- **Delayed sexual debut:** Research shows that delaying sexual activity leads to better health outcomes\n- **If sexually active:** Use condoms consistently and correctly; free condoms are available at government clinics\n- **Consent:** Both partners must freely agree. No means no. Consent cannot be given under the influence of alcohol or drugs.\n- **Know your rights:** The right to say no, the right to privacy, the right to protection from coercion\n\n**South African context:**\n- The legal age of consent is 16 years\n- Sex with a person under 16 is a criminal offence under the Sexual Offences Act\n- Teen pregnancy rates in SA remain high and significantly impact girls\' education and future opportunities'),
  t(9, '### Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs) and HIV/AIDS\n\nSTIs are infections transmitted through sexual contact:\n\n| STI | Key facts |\n|-----|-----------|\n| **Chlamydia** | Often has no symptoms; can cause infertility if untreated; treatable with antibiotics |\n| **Gonorrhoea** | Causes painful urination and discharge; treatable with antibiotics |\n| **Syphilis** | Starts with painless sores; can cause organ damage if untreated; treatable with penicillin |\n| **Herpes** | Causes painful blisters; no cure but can be managed with antiviral medication |\n| **HPV** | Can cause genital warts and cervical cancer; preventable with HPV vaccine |\n| **HIV/AIDS** | Weakens the immune system; no cure but manageable with antiretroviral therapy (ART) |\n\n**HIV/AIDS in South Africa:**\n- Approximately 8 million South Africans live with HIV\n- SA has the world\'s largest ART programme\n- HIV is transmitted through unprotected sex, sharing needles, mother-to-child transmission\n- HIV is NOT transmitted through casual contact (hugging, sharing food, toilet seats)\n- Free HIV testing and treatment are available at all government clinics\n- National AIDS Helpline: 0800 012 322\n\n**Reducing stigma:** People living with HIV can live long, healthy lives on ART. Stigma and discrimination discourage testing and treatment. Treat everyone with dignity and respect.'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following statements about HIV transmission is CORRECT?',
    ['HIV can be transmitted through unprotected sex, sharing needles, and mother-to-child during birth or breastfeeding', 'HIV can be transmitted through hugging someone who is HIV-positive', 'HIV can be transmitted by sharing food or drinking from the same cup', 'HIV can be transmitted through mosquito bites'], 0,
    'HIV is transmitted through specific body fluids: blood, semen, vaginal fluids, and breast milk. It CANNOT be transmitted through casual contact, sharing food, insect bites, or everyday activities like shaking hands or using the same toilet.'),
  fb(11, 'The legal age of sexual consent in South Africa is ___ years. The only 100% effective method of preventing STIs and pregnancy is ___.',
    ['16', 'abstinence'],
    'Under the Sexual Offences Act, the legal age of consent in South Africa is 16. Abstinence (choosing not to have sex) is the only method that is 100% effective in preventing both STIs and pregnancy.'),
];

