// CHAPTER 7: Healthy Lifestyle Choices (Term 3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Healthy Lifestyle Choices\n\nMaking healthy lifestyle choices during adolescence sets the foundation for a long and productive life. This chapter explores nutrition, substance abuse, physical fitness, and the values and strategies that support responsible decision-making.\n\n### Nutrition and a Balanced Diet\n\nNutrition is the process of providing your body with the food and nutrients it needs to function, grow, and repair itself.\n\n**The food groups:**\n\n| Food group | Function | Examples |\n|-----------|----------|----------|\n| **Carbohydrates** | Energy for daily activities and brain function | Bread, rice, pap, potatoes, pasta |\n| **Proteins** | Growth and repair of body tissues, muscle building | Meat, fish, eggs, beans, lentils, soya |\n| **Fats** | Energy storage, insulation, protection of organs | Oils, butter, nuts, avocado |\n| **Vitamins** | Regulate body processes, boost immunity | Fruits, vegetables (Vitamin C in oranges, Vitamin A in carrots) |\n| **Minerals** | Build bones and teeth, regulate fluids | Calcium (milk), iron (red meat, spinach) |\n| **Water** | Hydration, temperature regulation, waste removal | Water, fruit juice (limited), herbal tea |\n| **Fibre** | Aids digestion, prevents constipation | Whole grains, fruits, vegetables |'),
  t(2, '### Nutrition in the South African Context\n\nSouth Africa faces a unique "double burden" of malnutrition:\n\n**Undernutrition:**\n- Many children in low-income communities do not get enough nutritious food\n- Stunting (being too short for age) affects approximately 27% of children under 5\n- The National School Nutrition Programme (NSNP) provides free meals to over 9 million learners at no-fee schools\n\n**Overnutrition and obesity:**\n- Fast food, sugary drinks, and processed snacks are increasingly available and affordable\n- South Africa has rising rates of obesity, especially among women and in urban areas\n- Obesity increases the risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain cancers\n\n**Making healthier food choices:**\n- Eat a variety of foods from all food groups\n- Drink plenty of water — at least 6-8 glasses per day\n- Limit sugary drinks (cooldrinks, energy drinks)\n- Eat more fruits and vegetables — aim for 5 servings per day\n- Choose whole grains over refined grains (brown bread over white)\n- Reduce intake of processed foods, salt, and sugar\n- Cook at home using fresh ingredients when possible\n- Read food labels to understand what you are eating'),
  q(3, 'What is the "double burden" of malnutrition in South Africa?',
    ['Both undernutrition (hunger and stunting) and overnutrition (obesity) exist in the population', 'All South Africans eat too much food', 'South Africa has no nutritional problems', 'Only children are affected by malnutrition in South Africa'], 0,
    'South Africa uniquely faces both undernutrition (hunger, stunting, micronutrient deficiencies in low-income communities) and overnutrition (obesity, lifestyle diseases linked to processed foods and sedentary lifestyles). This is called the double burden of malnutrition.'),
  t(4, '### Substance Abuse\n\nSubstance abuse is the harmful use of drugs, alcohol, or other substances that leads to physical, psychological, or social problems.\n\n**Common substances abused by South African youth:**\n\n| Substance | Effects | Risks |\n|-----------|---------|-------|\n| **Alcohol** | Impaired judgement, slowed reactions, loss of inhibitions | Liver damage, addiction, accidents, violence, FASD in pregnancy |\n| **Tobacco/Nicotine** | Temporary relaxation, then craving; highly addictive | Lung cancer, heart disease, respiratory illness, premature ageing |\n| **Cannabis (dagga)** | Relaxation, altered perception, hunger | Memory problems, reduced motivation, mental health issues in developing brains |\n| **Methamphetamine (tik)** | Intense high, increased energy, euphoria | Severe addiction, psychosis, dental damage, aggression, weight loss |\n| **Nyaope/whoonga** | Intense euphoria followed by severe drowsiness | Extreme addiction, organ damage, HIV risk from shared needles |\n| **Inhalants (glue, petrol)** | Lightheadedness, hallucinations | Brain damage, organ failure, sudden death |\n\n**Why young people use substances:**\n- Peer pressure and desire to fit in\n- Curiosity and experimentation\n- Stress, anxiety, or depression\n- Escape from problems at home or school\n- Boredom and lack of positive activities\n- Exposure through family members who use substances'),
  fb(5, 'The harmful use of drugs, alcohol, or other substances is called substance ___. The government programme that provides free meals to learners at no-fee schools is called the National School ___ Programme.',
    ['abuse', 'Nutrition'],
    'Substance abuse refers to the harmful use of any substance that affects physical, mental, or social well-being. The National School Nutrition Programme (NSNP) provides free meals to over 9 million South African learners.'),
  t(6, '### Saying No to Substance Abuse\n\n**Strategies to avoid substance abuse:**\n- Make a personal commitment not to use drugs or abuse alcohol\n- Choose friends who share your values and do not use substances\n- Practise refusal skills: say "No" firmly and clearly, give a reason if you want, and walk away\n- Find healthy alternatives: sport, music, art, reading, volunteering\n- Talk to a trusted adult if you feel pressured\n- Understand the legal consequences: possession and sale of most drugs is illegal in South Africa\n\n**Where to get help if you or someone you know has a substance problem:**\n- SANCA (South African National Council on Alcoholism and Drug Dependence): 011 892 3829\n- Narcotics Anonymous: 083 900 6962\n- Alcoholics Anonymous: 0861 435 722\n- Lifeline: 0861 322 322\n- Your school counsellor or a trusted teacher\n\n**Legal framework:**\n- The Prevention of and Treatment for Substance Abuse Act (Act 70 of 2008) governs substance abuse prevention and treatment\n- The Drugs and Drug Trafficking Act criminalises the possession and distribution of illegal drugs\n- Selling or providing alcohol to anyone under 18 is a criminal offence under the Liquor Act'),
  q(7, 'Nomvula\'s older cousin offers her a tik pipe at a family gathering, saying "Just try it, one time won\'t hurt." What is the BEST response?',
    ['Refuse firmly, walk away, and tell a trusted adult about the offer', 'Try it once to satisfy her curiosity since her cousin said it is safe', 'Take it but not use it so her cousin does not feel rejected', 'Use it because her cousin is older and must know better'], 0,
    'Refusing firmly and telling a trusted adult is the safest response. Methamphetamine (tik) is highly addictive, and even one use can trigger dependence. The claim that "one time won\'t hurt" is false — addiction can begin with a single use.'),
  t(8, '### Physical Fitness and Recreation\n\nPhysical fitness is essential for overall health and well-being during adolescence:\n\n**Components of physical fitness:**\n\n| Component | Definition | Example |\n|-----------|-----------|----------|\n| **Cardiovascular endurance** | Ability of heart and lungs to supply oxygen during sustained activity | Running, swimming, cycling |\n| **Muscular strength** | Amount of force a muscle can produce | Push-ups, weight lifting |\n| **Muscular endurance** | Ability to sustain muscle activity over time | Planks, sit-ups, long-distance running |\n| **Flexibility** | Range of motion at a joint | Stretching, yoga, gymnastics |\n| **Body composition** | Ratio of fat to lean body mass | Maintained through balanced diet and exercise |\n\n**Benefits of physical activity:**\n- Strengthens heart, lungs, muscles, and bones\n- Helps maintain a healthy weight\n- Reduces stress, anxiety, and depression\n- Improves sleep quality\n- Boosts concentration and academic performance\n- Builds social skills through team sports\n- The WHO recommends at least 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous physical activity per day for adolescents'),
  t(9, '### Relationship Between Physical and Mental Health\n\nPhysical and mental health are deeply connected:\n\n**How physical activity improves mental health:**\n- Exercise releases endorphins — natural chemicals that improve mood\n- Regular activity reduces cortisol (the stress hormone)\n- Physical accomplishment builds self-esteem and confidence\n- Team sports provide social connection and a sense of belonging\n- Exercise improves sleep, which is essential for mental health\n\n**How poor physical health affects mental health:**\n- Lack of exercise can contribute to depression and anxiety\n- Poor nutrition affects brain function and mood\n- Substance abuse damages both physical and mental health\n- Chronic illness creates stress and emotional burden\n\n**Values that support healthy lifestyle choices:**\n- **Self-respect:** Valuing your body and health enough to make healthy choices\n- **Self-control:** Being able to resist temptation and delay gratification\n- **Responsibility:** Taking ownership of your health decisions\n- **Respect for others:** Not pressuring others into unhealthy behaviour\n- **Abstinence:** Choosing not to engage in risky behaviour (drugs, alcohol, unsafe sex)\n- **Right to say no:** Asserting your right to make your own choices\n- **Right to privacy:** Your health decisions are personal\n- **Right to protect yourself:** Taking action to stay safe and healthy'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following BEST explains the relationship between physical activity and mental health?',
    ['Exercise releases endorphins that improve mood and reduces cortisol that causes stress', 'Physical activity and mental health are completely unrelated', 'Exercise only benefits physical health and has no effect on the mind', 'Mental health improves only through medication, not exercise'], 0,
    'Exercise triggers the release of endorphins (feel-good chemicals) and reduces cortisol (stress hormone). This biological mechanism explains why regular physical activity is recommended as part of treatment for mild to moderate depression and anxiety.'),
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// CHAPTER 8: Careers — Work and the Economy (Term 3–4)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Careers — Work and the Economy\n\nThis chapter extends your career exploration by examining the diversity of jobs in the South African economy, research careers in different sectors, work settings, and important career qualifications and frameworks.\n\n### Diversity in Jobs\n\nSouth Africa\'s economy includes a wide variety of jobs across different sectors and skill levels:\n\n**Economic sectors:**\n\n| Sector | Description | Examples of jobs |\n|--------|-------------|------------------|\n| **Primary** | Extraction of raw materials from nature | Farming, mining, fishing, forestry |\n| **Secondary** | Manufacturing and processing of raw materials into finished products | Factory worker, welder, food processing, textile manufacturing |\n| **Tertiary** | Providing services to individuals and businesses | Teacher, nurse, lawyer, accountant, retail worker, police officer |\n\n**Formal vs. informal economy:**\n- **Formal economy:** Registered businesses with contracts, tax compliance, and labour law protections. Examples: working for a company, government, or registered business\n- **Informal economy:** Unregistered businesses and self-employment, often without contracts or benefits. Examples: street vendors, car guards, domestic workers (some), informal traders\n- In SA, millions of people depend on the informal economy for survival\n- The informal economy contributes approximately 6% of GDP but employs a much larger share of workers'),
  t(2, '### Research Careers in Different Sectors\n\nExploring careers in different economic sectors helps you understand the range of opportunities available:\n\n**Careers in the primary sector:**\n- Agricultural scientist, farm manager, mining engineer, marine biologist, environmental scientist\n- SA context: agriculture employs many people in rural areas; mining remains a major contributor to GDP\n\n**Careers in the secondary sector:**\n- Mechanical engineer, electrician, chemical engineer, quality controller, artisan, production manager\n- SA context: manufacturing is concentrated in Gauteng, KZN, and the Western Cape\n\n**Careers in the tertiary sector:**\n- Doctor, teacher, software developer, marketing manager, social worker, financial adviser, journalist\n- SA context: the tertiary sector is the largest employer and contributor to GDP\n\n**Emerging and growing career fields:**\n- Information technology (coding, cybersecurity, data science)\n- Renewable energy (solar panel technician, wind farm engineer)\n- Healthcare (growing demand due to population growth and disease burden)\n- Digital marketing and e-commerce\n- Environmental management and conservation\n\n**Declining career fields:**\n- Coal mining (shift to renewable energy)\n- Traditional retail (shift to online shopping)\n- Manual manufacturing (increasing automation)'),
  q(3, 'Which economic sector provides services to individuals and businesses and is the largest employer in South Africa?',
    ['The tertiary sector', 'The primary sector', 'The secondary sector', 'The informal sector'], 0,
    'The tertiary sector (services) includes careers in education, healthcare, finance, retail, IT, and government. It is the largest sector in South Africa in terms of both employment and contribution to GDP.'),
  t(4, '### Work Settings\n\nDifferent careers offer different work settings. Understanding these helps you choose a career that matches your preferences:\n\n**Indoor work settings:**\n- Office: accountant, administrator, software developer, lawyer\n- Hospital/Clinic: doctor, nurse, pharmacist, radiographer\n- School/University: teacher, lecturer, librarian\n- Factory/Workshop: engineer, artisan, technician\n- Retail: shop manager, cashier, visual merchandiser\n\n**Outdoor work settings:**\n- Farm: farmer, agricultural scientist, farm worker\n- Construction site: builder, civil engineer, surveyor\n- Nature/Conservation: ranger, marine biologist, environmental scientist\n- Emergency services: paramedic, firefighter\n\n**Mixed settings:**\n- Journalist, social worker, police officer, salesperson, architect\n\n**Considerations for work settings:**\n- Do you prefer working indoors or outdoors?\n- Do you want a desk job or a physically active role?\n- Are you comfortable working in different locations, or do you prefer a fixed workplace?\n- Do you want to work regular hours (9-5) or are you open to shift work, weekends, or travel?'),
  fb(5, 'Jobs in farming, mining, and fishing are in the ___ sector of the economy. The sector that provides services and is the largest employer in SA is the ___ sector.',
    ['primary', 'tertiary'],
    'The primary sector involves extraction of raw materials (farming, mining, fishing, forestry). The tertiary sector provides services (education, healthcare, finance, retail) and is the largest sector in South Africa.'),
  t(6, '### South African Qualifications Authority (SAQA) and the NQF\n\nSouth Africa has a structured qualifications framework to ensure quality and recognition of all qualifications:\n\n**SAQA (South African Qualifications Authority):**\n- An independent body established by law to oversee the National Qualifications Framework\n- Ensures that qualifications are of an acceptable quality\n- Evaluates foreign qualifications for use in SA\n\n**NQF (National Qualifications Framework):**\nThe NQF organises all South African qualifications on a single 10-level framework:\n\n| NQF Level | Qualification | Example |\n|-----------|--------------|----------|\n| **1** | Grade 9 (GET Certificate) | End of General Education and Training |\n| **2-4** | Grades 10-12 (NSC) and NCV | Matric, TVET N-courses |\n| **5** | Higher Certificate | 1-year qualification after matric |\n| **6** | Diploma / Advanced Certificate | 3-year diploma at UoT or TVET |\n| **7** | Bachelor\'s degree | 3-4 year degree at university |\n| **8** | Honours degree / Postgrad Diploma | 1 year after bachelor\'s |\n| **9** | Master\'s degree | 1-2 years of research/coursework |\n| **10** | Doctoral degree (PhD) | 3+ years of original research |\n\n**RPL (Recognition of Prior Learning):**\nRPL allows people who have gained knowledge and skills through work experience (but without formal qualifications) to have their competencies recognised. This opens doors for people who could not access formal education.'),
  q(7, 'Gogo MaNkosi has been a traditional healer for 30 years but has no formal qualifications. Which process could help her have her knowledge officially recognised?',
    ['Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL)', 'Applying for a matric certificate', 'Starting school in Grade 1 again', 'There is no way to recognise informal learning in SA'], 0,
    'Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) is a process that allows people to have knowledge and skills gained through work experience, informal learning, or community practice formally assessed and recognised within the NQF framework.'),
  t(8, '### Lifelong Learning\n\nIn today\'s rapidly changing world, learning does not stop when you leave school:\n\n**What is lifelong learning?**\nLifelong learning is the ongoing, voluntary pursuit of knowledge and skills throughout your life. It includes formal education, informal learning, and self-directed learning.\n\n**Why lifelong learning matters:**\n- Technology is changing jobs faster than ever — skills that are in demand today may be obsolete in 10 years\n- The South African economy needs workers who can adapt and upskill\n- Lifelong learning increases employability and career advancement\n- It keeps your mind active and engaged\n\n**Types of learning:**\n- **Formal learning:** Structured education at schools, universities, TVET colleges (leads to qualifications)\n- **Informal learning:** Learning through daily experiences, reading, watching videos, conversations\n- **Non-formal learning:** Organised learning outside the formal system (workshops, short courses, online courses)\n\n**The ability to change, re-train, and develop:**\n- Flexibility is one of the most important career skills in the 21st century\n- Online learning platforms (Coursera, Udemy, Khan Academy) make education accessible\n- Many South African employers offer in-service training and learnerships\n- SETA (Sector Education and Training Authorities) coordinate skills development in different economic sectors'),
  t(9, '### Opportunities in Careers\n\nThe world of work offers many different pathways and opportunities:\n\n**Work in different career fields including recreation, fitness, and sport industries:**\n- Personal trainer, sports coach, fitness instructor, sports journalist\n- Physiotherapist, sports psychologist, dietitian\n- Event manager, tourism officer, recreation facility manager\n- The SA fitness and wellness industry is growing rapidly\n\n**Skills required in modern careers:**\n- **Technical skills:** Specific to your career (e.g., coding, accounting, nursing procedures)\n- **Soft skills:** Communication, teamwork, problem-solving, time management, leadership\n- **Digital literacy:** Ability to use computers, software, and the internet effectively\n- **Critical thinking:** Analysing information and making sound decisions\n- **Adaptability:** Being flexible and willing to learn new skills\n\n**Salary packages, promotion possibilities, and further study:**\n- Salary depends on qualifications, experience, industry, and location\n- Entry-level positions pay less but provide essential experience\n- Promotion usually requires a combination of performance, experience, and further qualifications\n- Many careers offer paths for further specialisation through postgraduate study\n- Research salary ranges for careers you are interested in to set realistic expectations'),
  q(10, 'Why is lifelong learning important in the modern world of work?',
    ['Technology is changing jobs rapidly, so workers must continuously update their skills to remain employable', 'Employers do not care about qualifications or skills development', 'All jobs will remain exactly the same for the next 50 years', 'Learning is only important during school years and has no value afterwards'], 0,
    'The pace of technological change means that many jobs are being transformed or replaced. Workers who continuously learn and adapt are more employable and can take advantage of new opportunities. Lifelong learning is essential for career success in the 21st century.'),
  fb(11, 'The framework that organises all South African qualifications on 10 levels is called the ___. The bodies that coordinate skills development in different economic sectors are called ___.',
    ['NQF', 'SETAs'],
    'The National Qualifications Framework (NQF) provides a single framework for all South African qualifications from Grade 9 (Level 1) to doctoral degree (Level 10). SETAs (Sector Education and Training Authorities) coordinate skills development in specific economic sectors.'),
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// CHAPTER 9: Living in a Multi-Religious Society and Sport (Term 4)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch9_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Living in a Multi-Religious Society\n\nSouth Africa is home to people of many different religions and belief systems. The Constitution protects freedom of religion (Section 15) and freedom of belief and opinion, making it essential to understand and respect religious diversity.\n\n### Major Religions in South Africa\n\n| Religion | Key beliefs | Presence in SA |\n|----------|------------|----------------|\n| **Christianity** | Belief in God, Jesus Christ as saviour, the Bible as sacred text | Practised by approximately 80% of the population; many denominations (Catholic, Anglican, Methodist, Zionist, Charismatic) |\n| **Islam** | Belief in Allah, Prophet Muhammad, the Quran | Significant communities in the Western Cape (Cape Malay), Gauteng, and KZN |\n| **Hinduism** | Belief in Brahman (supreme reality), karma, dharma, reincarnation | Mainly practised in KwaZulu-Natal, brought by Indian indentured labourers in the 1860s |\n| **Judaism** | Belief in one God, the Torah, covenant with the Jewish people | Small but historically significant community, mainly in Gauteng and Western Cape |\n| **Buddhism** | Teachings of Siddhartha Gautama (the Buddha), Four Noble Truths, Eightfold Path | Small but growing community |\n| **Baha\'i Faith** | Unity of God, unity of religion, unity of humanity | Small community across SA |\n| **African Traditional Religions** | Belief in ancestors, the Supreme Being, ubuntu, connection to the land | Practised widely, often alongside Christianity |'),
  t(2, '### Ethical Teachings and Values Across Religions\n\nDespite their differences, most religions share common ethical values:\n\n**Shared values:**\n- **Respect for human life and dignity**\n- **Compassion and kindness** to others, especially the vulnerable\n- **Honesty and integrity** in all dealings\n- **Justice and fairness** — treating others as you wish to be treated\n- **Community responsibility** — caring for the less fortunate\n- **Forgiveness and reconciliation**\n\n**Respecting religious diversity:**\n- Learn about religions different from your own — knowledge reduces prejudice\n- Do not mock or belittle anyone\'s beliefs or practices\n- Understand that dietary rules, dress codes, and prayer times are important to adherents\n- Recognise that schools serve learners from many faith backgrounds\n- Religious freedom is protected, but no religion may be imposed on others\n\n**Challenges:**\n- Religious intolerance and discrimination\n- Tensions between traditional practices and constitutional rights\n- Exploitation by some religious leaders (commercialisation of faith)\n- Balancing religious freedom with the rights of others (e.g., dress codes in schools)'),
  q(3, 'Section 15 of the South African Bill of Rights protects:',
    ['Freedom of religion, belief, and opinion', 'Freedom of expression and speech only', 'The right to impose your religion on others', 'Freedom from having to learn about other religions'], 0,
    'Section 15 guarantees everyone the right to freedom of conscience, religion, thought, belief, and opinion. This means you may practise your religion freely, but you may not impose it on others.'),
  t(4, '### Sport and Nation Building\n\nSport plays a powerful role in South African society — uniting people across racial, cultural, and socio-economic divides.\n\n**How sport builds the nation:**\n- Creates a shared national identity (supporting the Springboks, Bafana Bafana, Proteas, Banyana Banyana)\n- Provides role models from diverse backgrounds\n- Promotes values of teamwork, discipline, respect, and fair play\n- Bridges racial and cultural divides — sport is a "universal language"\n- Contributes to the economy through tourism, broadcasting, and merchandise\n\n**Key moments in SA sports history:**\n- **1995 Rugby World Cup:** Nelson Mandela wearing the Springbok jersey united a divided nation. This moment symbolised reconciliation.\n- **1996 Africa Cup of Nations:** Bafana Bafana won on home soil, boosting national pride\n- **2010 FIFA World Cup:** The first World Cup on African soil showcased SA to the world\n- **2019 Rugby World Cup:** Siya Kolisi became the first Black captain to lift the Webb Ellis Cup, symbolising transformation\n- **Caster Semenya:** Olympic gold medallist who sparked global conversations about gender in sport\n- **Wayde van Niekerk:** Olympic gold and 400m world record holder from South Africa'),
  fb(5, 'Freedom of religion is protected by Section ___ of the Bill of Rights. In the 1995 Rugby World Cup, President ___ wore the Springbok jersey to symbolise national unity.',
    ['15', 'Mandela'],
    'Section 15 of the Bill of Rights protects freedom of religion, belief, and opinion. Nelson Mandela wore the Springbok jersey (previously a symbol of apartheid) at the 1995 Rugby World Cup final, symbolising reconciliation and national unity.'),
  t(6, '### Challenges in South African Sport\n\n**Transformation:**\n- Under apartheid, sport was racially segregated — Black, Coloured, and Indian athletes had inferior facilities and opportunities\n- Post-1994, transformation policies aim to ensure representation of all racial groups in national teams\n- Progress has been made, but challenges remain — access to quality coaching and facilities is still unequal\n\n**Gender inequality:**\n- Women\'s sport receives less media coverage, sponsorship, and funding than men\'s\n- Banyana Banyana (women\'s national soccer team) won the 2022 WAFCON but still receives far less support than Bafana Bafana\n- More women in coaching and sports administration positions is needed\n\n**Fairness and ethics in sport:**\n- Drug-taking (doping) gives athletes an unfair advantage and is banned\n- Match-fixing undermines the integrity of competition\n- Corruption in sports administration diverts resources from athletes and development\n- Subjective umpiring and bias can affect outcomes\n- Stereotyping: assumptions about which sports are suitable for certain races or genders limit participation'),
  q(7, 'Why was Siya Kolisi\'s captaincy of the Springboks in 2019 considered historically significant?',
    ['He was the first Black captain to lead the Springboks to a Rugby World Cup victory, symbolising transformation', 'He was the youngest player in the team', 'He scored the most tries in the tournament', 'He was the first South African to play rugby'], 0,
    'Siya Kolisi\'s captaincy was historically significant because he was the first Black player to captain the Springboks, and he led them to victory in the 2019 Rugby World Cup. This symbolised the progress of transformation in a sport that was once exclusively associated with White South Africans under apartheid.'),
  t(8, '### Incidence of Gender, Race, and Bias in Sport\n\n**Gender stereotyping in sport:**\n- Certain sports are still considered "male" (rugby, boxing) or "female" (netball, gymnastics)\n- These stereotypes limit participation and deny opportunities\n- In reality, people of all genders can excel in any sport\n\n**Racial stereotyping in sport:**\n- Historical assumptions about which racial groups suit certain sports persist\n- These stereotypes ignore individual talent and potential\n- Transformation policies in SA sport aim to address historical imbalances\n\n**Ways to address bias in sport:**\n- Ensure equal access to facilities, coaching, and funding across communities\n- Challenge stereotypes when they arise in conversation or media\n- Support diversity in sporting codes at school level\n- Recognise and celebrate achievements from all backgrounds\n- Report discrimination to the relevant sporting body or the SAHRC\n\n**Fair and unfair practices in sport:**\n\n| Fair practices | Unfair practices |\n|---------------|------------------|\n| Selection based on talent and performance | Selection based on race, gender, or connections |\n| Equal access to training and facilities | Unequal resource distribution |\n| Objective judging and umpiring | Subjective or biased officiating |\n| Anti-doping programmes | Drug use to enhance performance |\n| Sportsmanship and respect for opponents | Violence, intimidation, and cheating |'),
  q(9, 'Which of the following is an example of an unfair practice in sport?',
    ['Selecting players based on personal connections rather than talent and performance', 'Providing equal training facilities for all athletes regardless of background', 'Using objective criteria for judging performances', 'Implementing anti-doping programmes to ensure fair competition'], 0,
    'Selection based on personal connections (nepotism) rather than merit is an unfair practice that undermines the integrity of sport. Fair selection should be based on talent, performance, and potential.'),
  t(10, '### The Role of Sport in Your Life\n\nParticipating in sport and physical activity is beneficial regardless of your ability level:\n\n**Benefits of participating in sport:**\n- Physical fitness and health\n- Stress relief and improved mental health\n- Social connections and friendships\n- Learning discipline, commitment, and time management\n- Building self-confidence and resilience\n- Potential career opportunities in sport and fitness\n\n**Physical Education in the CAPS curriculum:**\nLife Orientation includes a practical Physical Education component where you:\n- Participate in activities that promote physical fitness\n- Understand safety issues related to physical activity\n- Demonstrate movement performance in various activities\n- Develop skills in individual, partner, and team activities\n\n**Getting involved:**\n- Join school sport teams or recreational activities\n- Participate in community sports leagues or clubs\n- Try indigenous South African games: jukskei, morabaraba, dibeke, drie stokkies\n- Focus on enjoyment and personal improvement rather than only competition\n- Remember: any physical activity is better than none — even walking counts!'),
  fb(11, 'The practice of using banned substances to gain an unfair advantage in sport is called ___. The practical physical activity component of the Life Orientation curriculum is called ___ Education.',
    ['doping', 'Physical'],
    'Doping is the use of prohibited performance-enhancing substances in sport, which is banned by organisations like WADA (World Anti-Doping Agency). Physical Education is the practical component of Life Orientation that focuses on fitness, movement, and sport participation.'),
];

